## PVE vs PAM Realms

The bootstrap creates users in two different realms:

**PAM (`user@pam`)** — Pluggable Authentication Modules, Linux system authentication. The user exists as
a real OS user on the Proxmox host. Can SSH, use sudo, access the shell. This is for human admins who
need GUI + shell access. Password managed at the Linux level (`passwd`).

**PVE (`user@pve`)** — Proxmox's internal user database only. Does NOT exist at the OS level — lives
only in Proxmox's ACL database (`/etc/pve/user.cfg`). Cannot SSH, cannot use Linux tools. Can only
interact through the Proxmox API or web GUI. This is exactly right for automation accounts like the
Terraform user — no shell access means if the API token leaks, the attacker can only make Proxmox API
calls, cannot pivot to the OS.

A third realm comes later: when FreeIPA is integrated, users authenticate through the directory — that's
a later chapter.

Design: PAM for human admin (needs GUI + shell), PVE for Terraform automation (API-only). Clean
separation.

