## The Bootstrap Token — Privilege Scope

The Terraform API token is created in the bootstrap with:

```bash
pveum acl modify "/" --users "${FULL_USER}" --roles Administrator
pveum user token add "${FULL_USER}" "${TOKEN_ID}" --privsep 0 --expire 0
```

Two important flags:

**`--privsep 0`** — privilege separation disabled. The token inherits ALL permissions of the parent user.
Since the parent user has the Administrator role on `/` (root of the ACL tree), this token has full admin
access to everything in Proxmox. If `--privsep 1` (the default), the token would need its own separate
ACL entries independent of the parent user — more secure but requires explicitly granting each permission
the token needs.

**`--expire 0`** — token never expires.

Combined: permanent full-admin key. This is the main gate, same design philosophy as the TerraformAdmin
role in AWS — broad authority, controlled at the access layer (who can trigger the workflow), not at the
permission layer.

**Important caveat discovered in practice:** `--privsep 0` does give the token the full permissions of
the parent user — there are no hidden ACL restrictions. What was hit during early testing was a different
problem: certain storage operations failed not because the token lacked privilege, but because the
bpg/proxmox Terraform provider doesn't implement every Proxmox API endpoint. Some storage-level and
cluster-level actions are either unsupported or use different API paths than what the provider calls.
The token had full authority — the tool didn't have the implementation.

Fetching the root SSH password and running CLI commands directly was tested as a workaround and worked,
but dropped — bypasses the API entirely and is messier and riskier than designing around the provider
limitation.

Worth keeping clear for interviews: API token scope and API provider coverage are two different things.
The token having Administrator privileges doesn't mean the TF provider can exercise every operation that
privilege allows.

